NNOOTTEESS
       The maximum useful size of a swap area depends on the architecture and
       the kernel version.

       The maximum number of the pages that is possible to address by swap
       area header is 4294967295 (32-bit unsigned int). The remaining space on
       the swap device is ignored.

       Presently, Linux allows 32 swap areas. The areas in use can be seen in
       the file _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_w_a_p_s.

       mmkksswwaapp refuses areas smaller than 10 pages.

       If you don’t know the page size that your machine uses, you can look it
       up with ggeettccoonnff PPAAGGEESSIIZZEE.

       To set up a swap file, it is necessary to create that file before
       initializing it with mmkksswwaapp, e.g. using a command like

           # dd if=/dev/zero of=swapfile bs=1MiB count=$((8*1024))

       to create 8GiB swapfile.

       Please read notes from sswwaappoonn(8) about tthhee sswwaapp ffiillee uussee rreessttrriiccttiioonnss
       (holes, preallocation and copy-on-write issues).

